<file path="docs/Human-OS-v2-Architecture.md">
  1: # Human OS v2 Architecture
  2: 
  3: Version: 2.0
  4: 
  5: Status: Living Document
  6: 
  7: ---
  8: 
  9: # Purpose
 10: 
 11: This document defines the long-term architecture of Human OS.
 12: 
 13: The goal is to ensure Human OS can evolve for years without constant restructuring.
 14: 
 15: This architecture should support millions of users, multiple AI models, mobile applications, enterprise deployments, plugins, and future autonomous capabilities.
 16: 
 17: ---
 18: 
 19: # Core Philosophy
 20: 
 21: Human OS is not a chatbot.
 22: 
 23: Human OS is an AI Operating System.
 24: 
 25: The LLM is one component.
 26: 
 27: The Human is the center.
 28: 
 29: Every architectural decision must strengthen one of these:
 30: 
 31: - Understanding
 32: - Memory
 33: - Planning
 34: - Execution
 35: - Trust
 36: 
 37: ---
 38: 
 39: # Architecture Layers
 40: 
 41: Human OS is divided into five major layers.
 42: 
 43: ## 1. App Layer
 44: 
 45: Responsible only for routing and page composition.
 46: 
 47: Examples:
 48: 
 49: - /chat
 50: - /dashboard
 51: - /onboarding
 52: - /settings
 53: 
 54: Rules:
 55: 
 56: - No business logic
 57: - No AI logic
 58: - No knowledge processing
 59: 
 60: The App Layer renders the experience.
 61: 
 62: ---
 63: 
 64: ## 2. Feature Layer
 65: 
 66: Features combine multiple Brain modules into complete user experiences.
 67: 
 68: Examples:
 69: 
 70: - Onboarding
 71: - Chat
 72: - Dashboard
 73: - Calendar
 74: - Tasks
 75: - Notes
 76: - Health
 77: 
 78: Each feature owns:
 79: 
 80: - UI
 81: - Local state
 82: - User flow
 83: 
 84: Features never own AI reasoning.
 85: 
 86: ---
 87: 
 88: ## 3. Brain Layer
 89: 
 90: The Brain is the intelligence of Human OS.
 91: 
 92: Modules include:
 93: 
 94: AI
 95: 
 96: Knowledge
 97: 
 98: Memory
 99: 
100: Profile
101: 
102: Mission
103: 
104: Planner
105: 
106: Reasoning
107: 
108: Execution
109: 
110: Evaluation
111: 
112: Role Engine
113: 
114: Every module has one responsibility.
115: 
116: Modules communicate through clean interfaces.
117: 
118: ---
119: 
120: ## 4. Shared Layer
121: 
122: Reusable components.
123: 
124: Examples:
125: 
126: UI Components
127: 
128: Hooks
129: 
130: Utilities
131: 
132: Types
133: 
134: Configuration
135: 
136: No business rules belong here.
137: 
138: ---
139: 
140: ## 5. Infrastructure Layer
141: 
142: Everything external.
143: 
144: Examples:
145: 
146: OpenRouter
147: 
148: OpenAI
149: 
150: Anthropic
151: 
152: Ollama
153: 
154: Supabase
155: 
156: PostgreSQL
157: 
158: Redis
159: 
160: Vector Databases
161: 
162: Cloud Storage
163: 
164: Infrastructure should be replaceable.
165: 
166: ---
167: 
168: # Brain Responsibilities
169: 
170: Knowledge
171: 
172: Stores trusted information.
173: 
174: Memory
175: 
176: Stores user history.
177: 
178: Profile
179: 
180: Stores identity.
181: 
182: Mission
183: 
184: Stores goals.
185: 
186: Planner
187: 
188: Creates plans.
189: 
190: Execution
191: 
192: Performs actions.
193: 
194: Evaluation
195: 
196: Measures quality.
197: 
198: Role Engine
199: 
200: Adapts Human OS behavior.
201: 
202: ---
203: 
204: # Design Rules
205: 
206: Every module must have one responsibility.
207: 
208: Every feature must improve the user's life.
209: 
210: No circular dependencies.
211: 
212: No duplicated logic.
213: 
214: The Brain never depends on UI.
215: 
216: UI depends on the Brain.
217: 
218: ---
219: 
220: # Data Flow
221: 
222: User
223: 
224: ↓
225: 
226: Feature
227: 
228: ↓
229: 
230: Brain
231: 
232: ↓
233: 
234: Infrastructure
235: 
236: ↓
237: 
238: Brain
239: 
240: ↓
241: 
242: Feature
243: 
244: ↓
245: 
246: User
247: 
248: The Brain remains the center of all intelligence.
249: 
250: ---
251: 
252: # Scalability Principles
253: 
254: Every module should be independently testable.
255: 
256: Every provider should be replaceable.
257: 
258: Every feature should be independently deployable.
259: 
260: Future mobile applications should reuse the Brain.
261: 
262: Future desktop applications should reuse the Brain.
263: 
264: ---
265: 
266: # Future Expansion
267: 
268: Human Graph
269: 
270: Long-Term Memory
271: 
272: Daily Planner
273: 
274: Autonomous Agents
275: 
276: Email Integration
277: 
278: Calendar Integration
279: 
280: Browser Integration
281: 
282: Voice Interface
283: 
284: Wearables
285: 
286: Home Automation
287: 
288: Enterprise Workspace
289: 
290: Plugins
291: 
292: ---
293: 
294: # Success Criteria
295: 
296: A new engineer should understand the architecture within one day.
297: 
298: A new feature should integrate without major refactoring.
299: 
300: Infrastructure providers should be replaceable with minimal code changes.
301: 
302: The architecture should remain understandable after years of development.
303: 
304: ---
305: 
306: # Living Document
307: 
308: This document is expected to evolve.
309: 
310: The principles should remain stable.
311: 
312: The implementation will continue to improve.
313: 
314: Every major architectural decision should be reflected here.
</file>

